DONE

  - The stamp is pager chrome, not a page line. It is carried on the
    document (tam_document_t.revision) rather than injected as a <p>, so
    it is absent from doc->lines, the footer line total, the attention
    line and search.
  - It is drawn on the row above the two footer rows, dim and
    right-aligned like the Showing <lens> line, on the normal background.
  - content_rows() reserves that row only when the page has a stamp and a
    breadcrumb; without a breadcrumb it uses the row already spare.
    Terminals shorter than five rows drop the stamp so the page keeps one
    line. A narrow terminal keeps the newest words after a leading
    ellipsis, falling back to tail characters.
  - tam_document_text() still prints the stamp once in the same position,
    so --pipe, the corpus build's reader check and tests/guide.sh keep
    working.
  - Renamed tam_depth_apply_rev() to tam_depth_prepare() because it no
    longer handles the revision.
  - Follow-up: fixed tests/test_documentation.py, whose libtam default
    still named the retired ~/.local/dev, so a bare make test-command
    failed before reaching the suite. It now uses the development prefix
    when that holds libtam, otherwise what pkg-config reports.


